Dear Reviewer eHSt,

Thank you so much for your reply. We sincerely appreciate your questions. They are beneficial for us to improve the quality of our paper. In order to make the paper clear. We remove the usage of the expectation and suppose the weight is constant directly.

Suppose there exists a $K$-component graph matrix  $\mathbf{W}\in\mathbb{R}^{N\times N}$ which is ordered correctly, and the $k$th component corresponds to the $k$th cluster $[c_{k-1}+1,c_{k}]$, where $c_{k-1}<c_{k},\forall k\in[1,K]$, and the dummy indexes $c_{0}:=0$, $c_{K}:=N$. Suppose the within-cluster weight of the $k$th cluster, $w_{ij}=\mu_{k}$, $\mu_{k}>0$, $\forall k\in[1,K]$, and the between-cluster weight, $w_{ij}=0$, $i\in[c_{k-1}+1,c_{k}]$, $j\notin[c_{k-1}+1,c_{k}], \forall k\in[1,K]$. Suppose the max weight is $\alpha$. Obviously, the min weight is $0$. We apply min-max normalization on all of the weights to make sure $w_{ij}\in[0,1]$ for any $i,j\in[1,N]$, i.e., the weight $w_{ij}$ is updated by $\frac{w_{ij}}{\alpha}$. 

Suppose we partition a $K$-component graph matrix into a two-block-diagonal form by index $t$. Then, the function $F(t)$ satisfies Theorem 3.1.
**Proof of Theorem 3.1**
Consider
\begin{align*}
F(t) &=\frac{\sum_{i,j\in[1,t]}\frac{w_{ij}}{\alpha}}{\sum_{i\in[1,t]}\sum_{j\in[1,N]}\frac{w_{ij}}{\alpha}}+\frac{\sum_{i,j\in[t+1,N]}\frac{w_{ij}}{\alpha}}{\sum_{i\in[t+1,N]}\sum_{j\in[1,N]}\frac{w_{ij}}{\alpha}} \\
&=\frac{\sum_{i,j\in[1,t]}w_{ij}}{\sum_{i\in[1,t]}\sum_{j\in[1,N]}w_{ij}}+\frac{\sum_{i,j\in[t+1,N]}w_{ij}}{\sum_{i\in[t+1,N]}\sum_{j\in[1,N]}w_{ij}} 
\end{align*}


**Case 0:** $K=1$, we have $F(t)=\frac{t^{2}\mu_{1}}{tN\mu_{1}}+\frac{(N-t)^{2}\mu_{1}}{(N-t)N\mu_{1}}=1$.

**Case 1:** $K\geq2$. Consider $t\in(0,c_{1}]$, we have
\begin{align*}
	F(t) & =\frac{t^{2}\mu_{1}}{tc_{1}\mu_{1}}+\frac{(c_{1}-t)^{2}\mu_{1}+(c_{2}-c_{1})^{2}\mu_{2}+...+(N-c_{K-1})^{2}\mu_{K}}{(c_{1}-t)c_{1}\mu_{1}+(c_{2}-c_{1})^{2}\mu_{2}+...+(N-c_{K-1})^{2}\mu_{K}}\\
	& =\frac{t}{c_{1}}+\frac{1+(c_{1}-t)^{2}C_{1}}{1+(c_{1}-t)c_{1}C_{1}}
\end{align*}
where $C_{1}=\frac{\mu_{1}}{(N-c_{1})^{2}b(2,K)}$ and $b(2,K)=\frac{(c_{2}-c_{1})^{2}\mu_{2}+...+(N-c_{k-1})^{2}\mu_{K}}{(N-c_{1})^{2}}$. Then, we have
\begin{align*}
	F(t)
	& =\frac{t(1+(c_{1}-t)c_{1}C_{1})+c_{1}(1+(c_{1}-t)^{2}C_{1})}{c_{1}(1+(c_{1}-t)c_{1}C_{1})}\\
	& =\frac{t+c_{1}+(c_{1}-t)c_{1}C_{1}(t+c_{1}-t)}{c_{1}+(c_{1}-t)c_{1}^{2}C_{1}}\\
	& =\frac{t+c_{1}+(c_{1}-t)c_{1}^{2}C_{1}}{c_{1}+(c_{1}-t)c_{1}^{2}C_{1}}\\
	& =1+\frac{1}{(1+c_{1}^{2}C_{1})c_{1}t^{-1}-c_{1}^{2}C_{1}}
\end{align*}
$F(t)$ is monotonically increasing for $t\in(0,c_{1}]$.

**Case 2:** $K\geq2$. Consider $t\in[c_{K-1},N)$, we have
\begin{align*}
	F(t) & =\frac{(N-t)^{2}\mu_{1}}{(N-t)(N-c_{K-1})\mu_{1}}
	 +\frac{c_{1}^{2}\mu_{1}+(c_{2}-c_{1})^{2}\mu_{2}+...+(c_{K-1}-c_{K-2})^{2}\mu_{K-1}+(t-c_{K-1})^{2}\mu_{K}}{c_{1}^{2}\mu_{1}+(c_{2}-c_{1})^{2}\mu_{2}+...+(c_{K-1}-c_{K-2})^{2}\mu_{K-1}+(t-c_{K-1})(N-c_{K-1})\mu_{K}}\\
	& =\frac{(N-t)^{2}\mu_{1}}{(N-t)(N-c_{K-1})\mu_{1}}+\frac{c_{K-1}^{2}b(1,K-1)+(t-c_{K-1})^{2}\mu_{K}}{c_{K-1}^{2}b(1,K-1)+(t-c_{K-1})(N-c_{K-1})\mu_{K}}\\
	& =\frac{N-t}{N-c_{K-1}}+\frac{1+(t-c_{K-1})^{2}C_{2}}{1+(t-c_{K-1})(N-c_{K-1})C_{2}}\\
\end{align*}
where $C_{2}=\frac{\mu_{K}}{c_{K-1}^{2}b(1,K-1)}$ and $b(1,K-1)=\frac{c_{1}^{2}\mu_{1}+(c_{2}-c_{1})^{2}\mu_{2}+...+(c_{K-1}-c_{K-2})^{2}\mu_{K-1}}{c_{K-1}^{2}}$. Then, we have
\begin{align*}
	F(t) 
	& =1+\frac{N-t}{N-c_{K-1}+(N-c_{K-1})^{2}C_{2}(t-c_{K-1})}\\
	& =1+\frac{1}{(N-c_{K-1})(1+(N-c_{K-1})^{2}C_{2})(N-t)^{-1}-(N-c_{K-1})^{2}C_{2}}
\end{align*}
$F(t)$ is monotonically decreasing for $t\in[c_{K-1},N)$.
